---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.3 — String Traversal: The ScrollCipher &amp; AP String Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.8, 2.10 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> String traversal is one of the
    highest-weight topics on the AP Computer Science A exam. Because <code>charAt()</code> and the
    <code>Character</code> class are <strong>not</strong> on the
    <a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a>, every AP String algorithm
    uses a loop over indices combined with <code>substring(i, i + 1)</code>, <code>indexOf</code>,
    <code>length()</code>, and <code>equals</code>.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Starting Files (Download Both):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/ScrollCipher.java" download>ScrollCipher.java</a> — bare-minimum
            compiling stub.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/ScrollCipherCheck.java" download>ScrollCipherCheck.java</a> —
            automated AP-style test suite (18 checks across 4 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>javac ScrollCipher.java ScrollCipherCheck.java
java ScrollCipherCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>countAdjacentDoubles(String spell)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Count how many times two identical adjacent characters appear in <code>spell</code>. For
        example, <code>"bookkeeper"</code> contains <code>"oo"</code>, <code>"kk"</code>, and
        <code>"ee"</code> (returns <code>3</code>), and <code>"aaa"</code> contains two adjacent
        pairs at indices <code>0–1</code> and <code>1–2</code> (returns <code>2</code>).
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 1: The Lookahead Out-of-Bounds Crash</h3>
        <p>
            To compare character <code>i</code> with character <code>i + 1</code> using the AP Java
            Quick Reference, you compare <code>spell.substring(i, i + 1)</code> with
            <code>spell.substring(i + 1, i + 2)</code>. Watch what happens if you use a standard
            <code>i &lt; spell.length()</code> loop header:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Put this in countAdjacentDoubles and run ScrollCipherCheck:
int count = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; spell.length(); i++) {
    if (spell.substring(i, i + 1).equals(spell.substring(i + 1, i + 2))) {
        count++;
    }
}
return count;</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>When <code>spell</code> is <code>"bookkeeper"</code> (length <code>10</code>), what
                is the value of <code>i</code> on the final iteration of
                <code>i &lt; spell.length()</code>, and what second argument gets passed into
                <code>spell.substring(i + 1, i + 2)</code>?</li>
            <li>What must the loop bound be whenever your loop body inspects both index
                <code>i</code> and index <code>i + 1</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>reverseRunes(String scroll)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Return a new <code>String</code> containing the characters of <code>scroll</code> in
        reverse order (e.g., <code>"dragon"</code> becomes <code>"nogard"</code>).
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 2: Backward Traversal Boundaries</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If you walk backward from the end of <code>scroll</code>, why does starting at
                <code>int i = scroll.length()</code> and calling
                <code>scroll.substring(i, i + 1)</code> immediately throw
                <code>StringIndexOutOfBoundsException</code>?</li>
            <li>If your backward loop condition is <code>i &gt; 0</code> instead of
                <code>i &gt;= 0</code>, what exact character is missing from
                <code>reverseRunes("dragon")</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>shiftUppercaseCipher(String text, int shift)</code></h3>

    <p>
        Using the uppercase alphabet string:
    </p>
    <pre><code>String alpha = "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ";</code></pre>
    <p>
        For each 1-character substring <code>ch = text.substring(i, i + 1)</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Find its position <code>int pos = alpha.indexOf(ch);</code>.</li>
        <li>If <code>pos == -1</code> (spaces, punctuation, digits), append <code>ch</code>
            unchanged.</li>
        <li>If <code>pos &gt;= 0</code>, shift the position by <code>shift</code> (which may be
            positive, negative, or larger than 26!) wrapped into the range <code>0..25</code>, and
            append the shifted letter from <code>alpha</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap 3: How Java's <code>%</code> Operator Handles Negative Numbers</h3>
        <p>
            Suppose <code>ch</code> is <code>"A"</code> (<code>pos = 0</code>) and you are
            decrypting with <code>shift = -2</code>. You want to wrap backward 2 steps to
            <code>"Y"</code> (index <code>24</code>).
        </p>
        <ol>
            <li>What does Java evaluate <code>(0 + (-2)) % 26</code> to? (It is <strong>not</strong>
                <code>24</code>!) What happens when you pass that number into
                <code>alpha.substring(newPos, newPos + 1)</code>?</li>
            <li>Explain why the two-step normalization formula
                <code>int newPos = ((pos + shift) % 26 + 26) % 26;</code> guarantees a valid index
                in <code>0..25</code> for every possible positive or negative <code>shift</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · AP FRQ Synthesis Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>purgeCursedGlyph(String scroll, String glyph)</code></h3>

    <p>
        A classic AP CS A Free Response question asks you to remove every occurrence of a substring
        <code>glyph</code> from <code>scroll</code> using a <code>while</code> loop and
        <code>indexOf</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>While <code>scroll.indexOf(glyph)</code> is not <code>-1</code>:</li>
        <li>Slice out the occurrence at index <code>idx</code> by concatenating everything
            <em>before</em> <code>idx</code> (<code>scroll.substring(0, idx)</code>) with everything
            <em>after</em> the glyph (<code>scroll.substring(idx + glyph.length())</code>).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint — The Chained Formation Effect</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Look at Check 3 in Part 4 of <code>ScrollCipherCheck</code>:
                <code>purgeCursedGlyph("boook", "oo")</code>. On iteration 1, removing the first
                <code>"oo"</code> from <code>"boook"</code> leaves <code>"bok"</code> (only one
                <code>'o'</code> left). What if the input was <code>"b" + "o" + "oo" + "o" + "k"</code>
                or <code>"ababa"</code>? Trace <code>purgeCursedGlyph("boook", "oo")</code> step by
                step to verify why <code>idx + glyph.length()</code> slices at the exact right spot.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><code>java ScrollCipherCheck</code> reports <strong><code>18 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/string-loops.html">Loops + Strings</a> — traversal, reversing, substring patterns</li>
        <li><a href="../exam/java-quick-reference.html">Java Quick Reference</a> — String methods on the exam</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="4.3a" title="4.3 String Traversal: ScrollCipher &amp; AP String Build — AP CS A" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
